## 4.3 Scalability Notes

### Horizontal scaling of ingestion workers (10,000+ resume batches)
- **Competing consumers:** scale `ingestion-worker` replicas; each consumes from the shared
  `resume.ingestion.queue` with `prefetch=10`. With 20 replicas → 200 resumes in flight.
- **Queue partitioning:** for very large agencies, shard by agency using consistent-hash routing into
  `resume.ingestion.queue.{0..N}` so a single noisy tenant cannot starve others; workers subscribe to a
  subset of shards (consumer-group style).
- **Backpressure:** publisher monitors queue depth (management API) and throttles enqueue rate when depth
  exceeds a high-watermark, protecting downstream (embeddings/DB).
- **Stateless workers:** no local state → safe to autoscale on queue depth (KEDA RabbitMQ scaler in K8s).

### ChromaDB sharding strategy
- **Collection-per-job-profile** (`resumes_job_{id}`) is the primary isolation/sharding unit — bounded ANN
  search space and clean lifecycle per run.
- **Tenant isolation:** prefix collections by agency (`ag_{agencyId}__resumes_job_{id}`) and run separate
  ChromaDB instances per large tenant if needed.
- **Horizontal sharding:** route collections across multiple ChromaDB nodes via a deterministic hash of
  `job_profile_id`; a thin router in the RAG builder maps a profile to its node.
- **Lifecycle:** delete the collection when the job profile is closed to reclaim memory.

### OpenAI rate limit management at scale
- **Token bucket** (§3.3) caps request rate; a shared Redis-backed bucket coordinates the limit across all
  ranking-worker replicas (not just per-process).
- **Request queuing:** ranking requests beyond the bucket capacity wait rather than 429.
- **Batch API:** for 1000+ resumes prefer the Batch API (higher throughput, ~50% cheaper, async window).
- **Exponential backoff + jitter** on 429/5xx.
- **Model fallback:** on sustained throttling, fall back `gpt-4o → gpt-4-turbo` (or a smaller model for
  lower-priority profiles) via `OPENAI_FALLBACK_MODEL`.

### Socket.IO horizontal scaling
- **Redis adapter** (`@socket.io/redis-adapter`) broadcasts room events across all `socketio-server`
  replicas via Redis pub/sub, so a `ranking:result` emitted by any worker reaches every connected client.
- **Sticky sessions:** the load balancer (Kong / ALB) must pin a client to one node for the WS lifetime
  (cookie/IP hash) so the polling→WS upgrade handshake stays on one instance.
- **Room broadcast:** workers emit to `job:{id}` rooms; the adapter fans out cluster-wide.

